SCSS layers
A Quarto SCSS file is divided into layers by special comments. Each layer is spliced into a different point of the Bootstrap build:

/*-- scss:functions --*/
// helper functions, available to everything below

/*-- scss:defaults --*/
// Sass variable definitions, injected BEFORE Bootstrap's own defaults
$primary: #7287fd;
$body-bg: #1e1e2e;

/*-- scss:mixins --*/
// mixin definitions

/*-- scss:rules --*/
// plain CSS rules, appended AFTER Bootstrap
.custom-block { border-radius: 4px; }

The distinction that matters: variables belong in scss:defaults because Bootstrap’s own variables are declared !default and only take effect if nothing set them first. A $primary written in scss:rules is too late to influence anything Bootstrap computed from it. Conversely a selector written in scss:defaults lands before Bootstrap and gets overridden by it.
A file may contain any subset of the layers, in any combination.

The host guard — best value, near-zero cost
Load analytics only when the page is being served from the real domain:

(function () {
  if (location.hostname !== 'example.com') return;
  // ... load and configure the tag here ...
})();

Local dev servers, preview builds, and staging all have a different hostname, so they fall out automatically. Nothing to remember, nothing to re-check, nothing to configure in a console.
Do this at run time, not build time. A build-time flag requires your local build and your CI build to produce different output, which is more machinery and one more thing that can silently drift. A hostname check keeps one artifact behaving correctly wherever it is served.
The limitation is honest and worth stating: this excludes development traffic only. Visiting your own live site still counts, because the hostname matches there.

The per-device flag — covers your own devices
For the case the host guard cannot reach, keep a persistent opt-out in the browser and check it before the tag loads:

try {
  var q = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('analytics');
  if (q === 'off') localStorage.setItem('analytics-opt-out', '1');
  if (q === 'on')  localStorage.removeItem('analytics-opt-out');
  if (localStorage.getItem('analytics-opt-out')) return;
} catch (e) { /* storage unavailable — fall through and track */ }

Visit example.com/?analytics=off once in each browser you use. It survives IP changes, works on cellular, and works from any network — which is exactly where IP-based approaches fall apart.
Wrap the storage access in try/catch. localStorage throws rather than returning null in Safari private mode, and an uncaught throw in a &lt;head&gt; script takes out every script after it.
Its weaknesses: the flag is per browser profile, so private windows and any cleared-site-data event quietly resume counting you, and you have to remember the one-time visit on each new device.
This is often not worth building. Your own visits to your own live site collapse into a single returning user rather than inflating traffic, so the reports still answer “does anyone else read this”. Weigh that against a flag you must re-set in every browser.

Model Configuration

model specific attributes like sort and dist keys go in the model
configuration that applies to every model in a directory goes in dbt_project.yml
in-model config formatted like this


{{
    config(
      materialized = 'table',
      sort = 'id',
      dist = 'id'
    )
}}

Jinja

spaces inside delimiters. { this }, not {this}
newlines to separate logical blocks of jinja
indent 4 spaces into a jinja block so it’s clear the code is wrapped by it
don’t fuss over whitespace control. readable project code beats perfect compiled code


{% macro make_cool(uncool_id) %}

    do_cool_thing({{ uncool_id }})

{% endmacro %}


select
    entity_id,
    entity_type,
    {% if this %}

        {{ that }},

    {% else %}

        {{ the_other_thing }},

    {% endif %}
    {{ make_cool('uncool_id') }} as cool_id

Input
Say a resource named foo yields this one record:

{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "alpha",
  "bar": { "x": 10, "y": { "z": 99 } },
  "baz": ["p", "q"],
  "qux": [
    { "k": "a", "n": 1 },
    { "k": "b", "n": 2 }
  ]
}

bar is an object, baz is a list of scalars, qux is a list of objects.

